//===----------------------------------------------------------------------===//
// One SKU's memory hierarchy, for `vxc --machine fleet/b200.vx prog.vx`.
// See fleet/README.md for the shared space vocabulary and provenance policy.
//
// HBM figures verified 2026-08-03 against NVIDIA's DGX B200 specifications.
// On-die figures (L2, SMEM) remain unverified -- see fleet/README.md.
//
// Blackwell is a two-die package presented as one logical GPU. This models the
// package-level budget, which is what a single-GPU placement sees. Modelling
// the two dies and their NV-HBI link separately would need per-instance memory
// accounting, which #290 puts out of scope.
//
// Blackwell also adds tensor memory (TMEM), a distinct on-chip space alongside
// SMEM. It is deliberately NOT declared here: nothing in the reference program
// places into it yet, and a declared-but-unused space would appear in the
// hierarchy without any placement exercising it. Add it with the kernel that
// uses it.

// spec: NVIDIA HGX B200 per-GPU specifications, quoted verbatim from the
// Lenovo ThinkSystem HGX B200 180GB product guide's GPU specification table
// (verified 2026-08-03) -- GPU Memory "180 GB HBM3e", GPU Memory Bandwidth
// "7.7 TB/s", form factor SXM6.
//
// CAPACITY: 192 GB -- MEASURED ON HARDWARE, which outranks the spec sheets.
//   A B200 device reports 192 GB. Vendor materials say 180 GB (Lenovo's HGX
//   B200 per-GPU table; NVIDIA's DGX B200 aggregate of 1,440 GB / 8), which is
//   most likely a *usable* figure after ECC and reservation rather than a
//   different part.
//
//   A machine file should describe the machine, so it carries what the device
//   has. How much of it a deployment may consume is a *policy*, and belongs in
//   the admission program or the engine config -- not baked into the hardware
//   model, where it would be invisible and unadjustable.
//
//   THE CONSEQUENCE, WHICH IS NOT YET MODELLED: admitting against a bare
//   192 GB assumes a deployment can use all of it, and none can. vLLM's
//   default `gpu_memory_utilization` is 0.9, giving ~173 GB usable -- *below*
//   even the vendor's 180. So a config in roughly the 173-192 GB band will be
//   admitted here and still OOM in practice. That gap is exactly what #285's
//   `gpu_memory_utilization` field has to carry, and until it does, a verdict
//   near this SKU's ceiling should be read as "fits the device", not "fits a
//   serving deployment on the device". Neither 180 nor 192 is the right
//   admission bound on its own; the bound is capacity x utilization.
//
// BANDWIDTH: 7.7 TB/s chosen, but genuinely contested. UNRESOLVED.
//   Lenovo's per-GPU spec table:        7.7 TB/s
//   NVIDIA DGX B200 aggregate / 8:      8.0 TB/s  (64 TB/s over 8 GPUs)
//   Note 7.7 x 8 = 61.6, so NVIDIA's 64 may be a rounded-up aggregate, or the
//   two may describe different bin/power configurations. Not established.
//
//   7.7 is used because it is an explicit *per-GPU* figure rather than a
//   division, and because understating bandwidth is the safe direction: it
//   inflates the derived roofline cost, so a transfer looks more expensive
//   than it is rather than less. Revisit against the hardware the campaign
//   actually rents -- that measurement settles it better than any spec sheet.
//
//   (An earlier revision of this file claimed 8 TB/s "belongs to the 186 GB
//   GB200 NVL part". That was tidier than the evidence supports: NVIDIA's own
//   DGX B200 aggregate implies 8 TB/s for this 180 GB part too.)
Memory HBM {
  capacity: 192 GiB, bandwidth: 7.7 TB/s, managed: explicit, scope: device
}

// spec: NVIDIA Blackwell Architecture Technical Brief -- 126 MB L2 (two dies).
Memory L2 {
  within: Memory::HBM, capacity: 126 MiB, bandwidth: 16 TB/s, managed: cached, scope: device
}

// spec: CUDA C Programming Guide, compute capability 10.0 -- 228 KB
// configurable shared memory per SM.
Memory SMEM {
  // replicas: UNVERIFIED: B200 SM count, vendor figure
  // clock: UNVERIFIED: B200 boost clock, vendor figure
  within: Memory::L2, capacity: 228 KiB, bandwidth: 128 B/cyc, clock: 1.965 GHz, granule: 1 KiB, managed: explicit, scope: sm, replicas: 148
}

Topology Device {
  // spec: NVIDIA Blackwell GB100; LLVM's NVPTX target.
  arch: nvptx64,
  memory: Memory::HBM,
  visible: [Memory::HBM, Memory::L2, Memory::SMEM],
  // spec: NVIDIA RTX Blackwell PRO GPU Architecture whitepaper v1.0, "Blackwell 5th
  // Generation Tensor Cores": "The RTX Blackwell Tensor Cores support FP16, BF16, TF32,
  // INT8, and Hopper's FP8 Transformer Engine. RTX Blackwell adds new support for FP4 and
  // FP6 Tensor Core operations [...] similar to our datacenter-class Blackwell GPUs."
  // The FP4 row is what makes this field worth having: the same program refused on
  // h100-sxm is accepted here. FP6 has no ElementType, so it is not listed; nor does
  // INT4, which is emulated over int8 MMA on sm_100 rather than native.
  dtypes: [f64, f32, bf16, f16, f8e4m3, f8e5m2, f4e2m1, i8, u8, i16, u16, i32, u32, i64, u64, bool],
  // spec: PCIe Gen5 x16 = 63 GB/s per direction (32 GT/s x 16 lanes, 128b/130b).
  // UNVERIFIED on this SKU; a Grace/C2C-attached part reaches the host at ~450 GB/s instead,
  // a 7x difference in every host-seam prediction. Settling it is experiment M1/M4.
  // The on-die hops below declare no cost -- their `bandwidth:` figures derive it (E6013).
  transfer Memory::CPU_DRAM -> Memory::HBM : 63 GB/s,
  // The way home. Reading a placed tensor back is how a program says it still
  // holds it; without the edge the model can only describe one direction.
  transfer Memory::HBM -> Memory::CPU_DRAM : 63 GB/s,
  transfer Memory::HBM -> Memory::L2,
  transfer Memory::L2 -> Memory::SMEM
}
